  "explanation": "Sparse feature (nonzero on 1.7% of steps), active only at GO and only late in the episode (deciles 5-8, peak trials 70-79). The timing profile is used multiplicatively: the feature fires when the previous trial reached S1 and was rewarded and the policy favours a1 (choice logit > 0). Magnitude depends on how recently the agent switched to a1. It is largest right after a rare-rewarded a2 -> S1 trial (the model-based switch to a1, about 0.5). After that it decays over consecutive a1 choices: roughly halved one trial after the switch, a quarter two trials after, small on long a1 runs. When the window history is unknown, partial terms cover it. Unrewarded previous trials push it slightly below the timing baseline. Previous S2 outcomes, switches to a1 after a2 S2 u, value, the hidden good state and since_rev add nothing. A run count of consecutive S1 rewards (instead of a1-choice run length) scored worse (dev 0.26 vs 0.30). Using the future action==1 instead of choice>0 scores about the same (dev 0.29); choice>0 (the model policy at that step) is recorded. A Gaussian ep_trial shape was worse than the decile table. Dev R^2 beyond timing 0.30, test 0.32, above the additive all ceiling of 0.17 because the terms are multiplicative interactions. Big misses remain on the first a1 trials of a window, where the history needed to time the switch is not available.",
